WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.626
We established earlier that MAC tables store the information needed to direct traffic from a switch to a device.

00:06.626 --> 00:11.765
When a device connects to a switch, the MAC address of that device is learned.

00:11.765 --> 00:20.255
This way, if another device transmits data, the switch does one of two things: either it knows the MAC address of the destination and sends the data there,

00:20.255 --> 00:27.799
or it floods the data out of the other ports to the other devices to find out which device has the destination MAC address.

00:27.799 --> 00:37.645
As far as transmitting the actual data, switches use two main methods for doing so. Store-and-forward—this is an older method of sending data.

00:37.645 --> 00:45.041
Basically, a switch will receive a data frame in its entirety, check for errors, and then forward the frame.

00:45.041 --> 00:52.996
While this is reliable, it is also slower, as a frame isn't sent until its entire contents have been received and checked.

00:52.996 --> 00:57.311
The other and newer switch type is the cut-through method.

00:57.311 --> 01:05.167
In this method, a switch just needs to know the frame header that has the destination MAC address and then it sends the frame.

01:05.167 --> 01:15.564
While this is faster, this doesn't check for errors, so corrupt data frames can be sent, which are then just discarded at the destination.

